import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Actors",
	description: "Actors on the Roc Solid server platform: state that handles one message at a time, in the order they came, with typed methods, asks, tells and timers, its state and timers kept in the database so they outlive a restart.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "kinds", label: "Kinds and methods" },
		{ id: "asking", label: "Asking and telling" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · actors"
				title="State one message at a time"
			>
				A cart, a counter, a game's room: that is an actor, a piece of state that handles one message at a
				time, in the order they came, kept in the database so it is there after a restart. A hundred
				requests may change it at once, and none loses another's update. Who is here right now, and what
				was just said, is lighter: <a href={Paths.platform_live}>live state</a>, in memory.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="kinds">Kinds and methods</Heading>
			<p>
				An actor is a kind and a key: the cart of member 7, the counter called <code>visits</code>. A kind
				says its name and its state when it is new (<code>initial</code>, from its key). What its actors do
				is its methods (<code>Actor.method</code>), each with its own input and its own reply, typed: a turn
				of one is given the actor (<code>me</code>), the app's context (what its <code>init!</code> made),
				the actor's state and the input, and answers the next state and the reply.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_counter.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_counter.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nobody makes one, or ends one.</strong> A call to any key finds its actor as its last
					turn left it, or new: <code>initial(key)</code> is where its state starts, not a call.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One turn at a time, in the order they came.</strong> Each actor has a mailbox in the
					server, and takes what is in it one turn after another, whichever method each is: a hundred asks
					at once each see the state the one before left, with no lost updates and no locks to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A call it doesn't take is an error, not a crash.</strong> Methods are typed, so this is
					rare (a caller built against an older or newer kind: an input the method doesn't take, or a
					method it doesn't have); it, a crash in a method, and a kind the app has none of are logged as
					errors and told to the asker. The actor's state stays as it was, and it goes on with its next
					turn.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept in the database</strong> (<code>rocx_actors</code>), read by its first turn and written
					after each turn that changes it. One idle for 15 minutes (<code>--actor-idle-ms</code>) is let
					go, and the next call wakes it as the database has it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> lists the methods an app's modules define (<code>Actor.method</code>, at the
				top of a module's block; and checks its kinds, <code>Actor.define</code>, there too, each
				<code>name</code> first and its own), so the server can run an actor when nobody is waiting on it:
				a tell, a timer. Two methods of one kind with one name stop the server as it starts.
			</p>

			<Heading id="asking">Asking and telling</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ask.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ask.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Actor.ask!(method, key, input)</code> waits for the reply, typed as the method says, or
				<code>ActorErr</code> with why there is none. <code>Actor.tell!(method, key, input)</code> doesn't
				wait: its turn comes after the ones before it, and its reply goes nowhere.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_tell.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_tell.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				An actor may ask another. One that asks itself, or any actor whose turn is waiting on its answer,
				is refused at once: it would wait forever. An ask not answered in 30 s is given up on, and its
				call is dropped if its turn hasn't come.
			</p>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				A method is given the actor itself (<code>me</code>): its key (<code>Actor.key_of(me)</code>), and
				its own timers. <code>Actor.after!(me, ms, method, input)</code> calls one of its kind's methods
				later, and answers the timer, which <code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops. A timer every so often is one
				that sets the next (a method that names itself is a function of <code>{"{}"}</code>: a value can't
				be defined in terms of itself):
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_ticker.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_ticker.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Actor.tell_after!(method, key, ms, input)</code> does the same from anywhere: a request, a
				job, another actor.
			</p>
			<p>
				With a database, a timer outlives a restart: it is a row (<code>rocx_actor_timers</code>), written
				at once (outside any transaction the caller has open, as a tell is sent at once) and deleted when
				the turn that handled it has ended, and the server sets every row again as it starts. Its call is
				made at least once: a server killed in the middle of that turn makes it again after its restart.
				<code>Actor.cancel!</code> stops a timer that is waiting, or one that has fired whose turn hasn't
				come. Without a database, timers are in memory, and a restart loses them.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>An actor's state is JSON</strong> in one row, written after each message that changes it: fine for a cart, not for a ledger.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Tells are in memory.</strong> A restart loses those not yet handled: a job is for work
					that must survive one. Timers outlive it, with a database.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Rooms, topics, channels, values in memory, actors, tables and jobs, side by side:
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#which"}>which to use</a>.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests ask an actor a hundred times at once, crash one, have one ask another, itself
					and one waiting on it, tell one in order, set and cancel timers, send one a message it doesn't take,
					let one go when idle and wake it, and restart the server to find an actor's state where it was and
					its timer told once after the start.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
